  "response": "Adjudication between doctrinal and political explanations of a religious movement's expansion begins with the prior question of how fast, and along what curve, the movement actually grew. Because sociological accounts of conversion emphasize recruitment through pre-existing kinship and friendship networks, Rodney Stark was led to estimate that the early Christian population grew at roughly 40 percent per decade. Applied backward from the fourth century, that figure indicates that Christians made up roughly ten percent of the Roman Empire's population by 300 CE.\n\nThe force of such a reconstruction depends on assumptions that must be made explicit before any causal verdict is reached. Either Stark's model applies a constant rate of growth across every decade of the period, or it applies a rate of growth that varies from decade to decade; one of the two must be the case, and they cannot both hold. Roman imperial census records supply direct annual counts of Christians across the provinces, and the existence of such direct enumeration cannot be squared with treating dated tombstones as the best available index of how far the movement had reached. Dated Christian funerary inscriptions serve scholars as a proxy for the movement's geographical spread, and the availability of that proxy has led historians to test patronage explanations by searching for inflection points in growth curves after the date of state support.\n\nRamsay MacMullen argued that imperial favor was decisive for Christianization after 312 CE, and it is that very search for inflection points in the growth curves after the date of state support which shows his thesis to be correct. The documentary basis for weighing that thesis is the sequence of imperial measures themselves. Constantine granted Christian clergy exemption from compulsory civic obligations. The Edict of Milan of 313 CE made Christianity the official religion of the Roman Empire. The Edict of Thessalonica of 380 CE established Nicene Christianity as the official faith of the Roman Empire, an enactment that came decades after the clerical exemptions, and one that cannot both stand and allow the settlement of 313 to have already conferred official status on the religion.\n\nBy contrast with that legislated establishment, Manichaeism functions in the literature as a comparative case of a movement that expanded without durable imperial backing. Even granting the force of that comparison, the decisiveness that MacMullen ascribed to imperial favor after 312 is not thereby overturned, since the Manichaean diffusion was neither as rapid nor as institutionally entrenched as the Christian one. What settles disputes of this kind, then, is not the testimony of apologists or of hostile observers but the fit between a dated series of material remains and a model of growth whose parameters can be stated numerically and tested against the chronology of state action.",
